{htxt id="entity_permission"}
  <p>{ts}Set the permission level needed to view this entity.{/ts}</p>
  <p>{ts}Users without this permission will not be able to see this entity in SearchKit nor in any search queries or displays that use this entity.{/ts}</p>
{/htxt}
{htxt id="data_mode"}
  <p>{ts}Determine which data-storage features to use when generating this entity.{/ts}</p>
  <ul>
    {* We should probably give some more hints about when to use each. Included draft notes about expected trade-offs. *}

    {* CONCEPTS: As we explore trade-offs, we reference some usage patterns:
      * "Stable data" vs "Volatile data" -- How frequently do the underlying data-records change?
        For example, "Country Names" are stable, but "Queue Statuses" are volatile.
      * "Stable structure" vs "Volatile structure" -- How frequently do we change the columns or filters?
        For example, when a developer provides a pre-packaged search... that has stable structure.
        But when a novice user builds a custom search... that has volatile structure.
      * "Targetted access" -- You typically request 1-4 records at a time.
      * "Analytics with..." -- You typically synthesize data about all the records.
    *}

    {* TABLE: Expected trade-offs:
      Strength: Handling stable data and stable structures. Analytics with grouping/aggregation/function-evaluation.
      Weakness: Handling volatile data and volatile structures.
    *}
    <li>{ts}<strong>MySQL Table</strong>: Create a persistent MySQL table and re-fill it periodically.{/ts}</li>

    {* VIEW: Expected trade-offs:
      Strength: Handling volatile data with stable structures. Simple variations on existing entities. Targeted access to subsets of data.
      Weakness: Handling volatile structures. Analytics with grouping/aggregation/function-evaluation.
    *}
    <li>{ts}<strong>MySQL View</strong>: Create a persistent MySQL view. Better suited to volatile data with a stable data-structure. Better suited to targeted data-access but not to analytics.{/ts}</li>

    {* CTE: Expected trade-offs:
      Strength: Handling volatile data and volatile structures.
      Moderate: Analytics with grouping/aggregation/function-evaluation.
      Weakness: Targeted access to subsets of data.

      Compared to VIEWs, a key difference is CTEs are likely to build an on-demand temporary table to cache intermediate
      results. This is good if you need full-table operations like grouping/aggregating. But it may be heavy-handed if you're
      only accessing a small portion of the data.
    <li>{ts}<strong>MySQL CTE</strong>: Create dynamic MySQL query using Common Table Expression. Better suited to volatile {/ts}</li>
    *}
  </ul>

{/htxt}
